/*
  Camera 01 - Poly Haven, CC0, https://polyhaven.com/a/Camera_01
  Rajil Jose Macatangay. 26,987 triangles, 4 PBR materials.

  This is one self-contained glTF: the 845 KB vertex buffer and all nine 320px
  PBR maps are inlined as data URIs, the whole JSON is gzipped, and the gzip is
  base64. 1,357 KB of source becomes 588 KB gzipped, 784 KB as this string.

  Why not just fetch a .glb? Because a page opened over file:// cannot. Chrome
  treats a sibling file as cross-origin and blocks fetch() and XMLHttpRequest
  outright; a classic <script src> is the one loader still allowed. This demo
  has to run from disk with no server, so the model rides in as a string and is
  inflated at runtime with DecompressionStream('gzip').

  IN PRODUCTION, DO NOT DO THIS. Ship a Draco-compressed .glb with KTX2
  textures behind a CDN and call loader.load(). Poly Haven's own 1k textures
  were downsampled to 320 and every float in the vertex buffer had its low 13
  mantissa bits zeroed - invisible at this scale, and it is what makes the
  buffer gzip at all (raw float32 does not compress; rounded float32 does,
  by 38%% here).
*/
window.__MODEL_GZ_B64 = "";
